  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1115 AM MST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nRain and snow will spread across central and eastern Idaho in\nthe next 24-30 hours, and exit our area Wednesday morning.\nProgression initially will be a bit slow, with the bulk of\nmoisture falling over the central mountains south into northwest\nUtah and Nevada before sunrise...although some light\nprecipitation may push to near I-15. By sunset Tuesday, the main\nband of precipitation will extend from near Malta through Monida\nPass and east from there. An area of lingering precipitation\nwill extend back across the central mountains, especially along\nand northeast of US 93. By Wednesday morning, it will confined\nto generally the eastern highlands. Current trends limit\nportions of the Magic Valley, Wood River Valley, and\nChallis/Pahsimeroi Valleys to around 0.10\" or less. For most of\nrest of the Magic Valley, most of the Snake Plain, and remaining\nlower valleys...the latest forecast is in the 0.10-0.33\" range. \nFor areas north and east of Idaho Falls, look for up to 0.50\"\ndown low. For our mountain ranges, the latest forecast has\ntrended (like other area) to 0.30-0.65\". We are looking for\nlocally higher amounts around Pomerelle, the eastern highlands,\nand from Banner Summit north and west. In terms of snow, the\nforecast remains very tricky...especially for lower valleys and\nit is the usual race between colder air moving in and\nprecipitation end. Right now, our warmer and lower valleys have\nlittle to no accumulation. This makes sense as we do have warm\nground temperatures, and that \"battle\". We would not be\nsurprised to at least a mix or wet snow falling and melting\nwhere accumulations are lowest. We will need to watch, at least\nin the Snake Plain, for higher accumulations toward Rexburg and\nSt. Anthony where we may see 1-3\". For the mountains, we are\nlooking at 2-6\" as a general forecast, with 6-8\" above 8000ft\n(and mostly above 9000ft) across the Bear River Range, eastern\nhighlands, central mountains, and around Pomerelle. This will be\na wetter snow, so any wind really won't create additional\nimpacts. Where we will see impacts (heavier snowfall rates\nleading to slick roads), we have issued WINTER WEATHER\nADVISORIES. This includes areas above 7000ft in the Sawtooths,\nPioneers, Boulders (this evening through tomorrow morning), and\nin the southeast corner in the Bear River Range (tomorrow\nafternoon and night). We also issued an advisory for the Island\nPark area and the Big Holes. There will likely some travel\nimpacts across Island Park and higher passes for both areas.\nThat advisory is for tomorrow afternoon and night. There is\nstill some room for error as we have been mentioning for a few\ndays. Some of the new higher resolution models and AI versions\nare cranking out an additional 0.20-0.5\" in general with even\nmore in some spots. \n\nWe should a short break later Wednesday, but we quickly return\nto occasional rounds of precipitation through early next week.\nTemperatures are expected to remain on the cool/cold side, so\nthe potential for lower elevation snow is coming up. For a lack\nof a better term to use, trying to produce any definitive rain \nor snow amounts beyond Wednesday is futile. With split flow and\nany track of stronger areas of low pressure, that is to be\nexpected.",
